## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner's practical handling of untrusted input, safe logging, and data leakage risk in a common feature.

## Key Concepts

- JSON schema validation with no extraProperties
- Safe rendering with HTML escaping
- Logging hygiene and redaction
- Parameterized SQL queries and minimal data retention
- Basic test coverage for invalid input and log safety

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    message: { type: "string" },
    meta: { type: "object", properties: {}, additionalProperties: false }
  },
  required: ["message"],
  additionalProperties: false
};
if (!ajv.validate(schema, req.body)) {
  res.status(400).send("Invalid payload");
  return;
}
function sanitize(s){
  return String(s)
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&#39;");
}
const safeMessage = sanitize(req.body.message);
db.query("INSERT INTO messages (message) VALUES ($1)", [safeMessage]);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for edge cases like extremely large JSON or nested payloads?
- How would you adapt this plan for real-time content rendering in a multi-tenant app?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical security knowledge, understanding of XSS types, and real-world remediation strategies - critical for Fortinet's security products.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will explain: 1) Input validation vs output encoding, 2) CSP implementation, 3) The key difference in attack scope between reflected (single user) vs stored (all users), 4) Server-side vs client-side remediation approaches.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Fix: HTML encode output
function sanitizeSearchTerm(term: string): string {
  return term.replace(/[&<>"]/g, (match) => {
    const escape: Record<string, string> = {
      '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;'
    }
    return escape[match]
  })
}

// CSP header
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Security-Policy', "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'")
  next()
})
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this fix for bypasses?
- What if the search term appears in JavaScript context?
- How does CSP help prevent XSS even with imperfect sanitization?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical secure file upload handling, a common real-world area accessible to beginners. It demonstrates concrete mitigation steps rather than theoretical knowledge.

## Key Concepts

- File-type validation using magic bytes
- Size limits and safe storage
- Safe filename handling and path traversal protection
- Non-executable upload directory and proper permissions
- Basic CI checks for upload validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch for safe image upload checks
function isSafeImage(file) {
  const sizeLimit = 1024 * 1024;
  if (file.size > sizeLimit) return false;
  const header = new Uint8Array(file.slice(0, 4));
  const magic = (header[0] << 24) | (header[1] << 16) | (header[2] << 8) | header[3];
  const isPng = magic === 0x89504E47;
  const isJpeg = magic === 0xFFD8FFE0;
  if (!(isPng || isJpeg)) return false;
  const mime = file.type;
  if (mime !== 'image/png' && mime !== 'image/jpeg') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test with fuzzed inputs and edge cases?
- How would you integrate a lightweight virus scan in CI and production tooling?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design zero-trust data access for a high-scale system, focusing on data protection, auditability, and runtime governance.

## Key Concepts

